<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">
        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS" android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions: none, deliberately.

         Capacitor's scaffold asks for INTERNET, and the game has no use for
         it. Everything it loads — the page, the drawings, all 458 voice clips
         — is bundled in the APK and served by Capacitor's own local handler,
         which never touches the network. Nothing is fetched, reported or
         phoned home.

         The merged manifest is not quite empty, and that is expected:
         androidx.core adds a DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION named
         after our own applicationId, at signature protection level, to keep a
         receiver it registers unreachable by other apps. It grants nothing and
         requests nothing from the user. No android.permission.* survives the
         merge — check with `aapt2 dump permissions` on the release APK.

         Leaving the permission in would have been harmless at runtime and
         wrong everywhere else: Play lists it on the store page, and a
         children's app that claims to collect no data reads better when the
         manifest cannot ask for any. If a future feature genuinely needs the
         network, the Data Safety declaration and the privacy policy at
         lionforce.com.au/privacy/alphabet-match have to change with it. -->
</manifest>
